From: CJ Damsleth <kim@damsleth.no>
Subject: [PATCH DEBUG] nvme: apple: run completions in a threaded IRQ

T6040 ANS firmware 26.5 asserts at Linux's first I/O completion-queue
wrap, while m1n1 survives multiple wraps after matching the queue depth,
phase convention, tag value, TCB slot, interrupt-enabled CQ, and immediate
CQ-head doorbell updates.  The remaining structural difference is that
Linux handles completions in hard-IRQ context while m1n1 polls
synchronously.

Request the existing nvme-apple handler as an IRQF_ONESHOT thread.  This
keeps the handler body, CQ/TCB ordering, MMIO addresses and values, queue
formats, and storage commands unchanged; it changes only the execution
context and interrupt-line masking around the handler.

This is a diagnostic discriminator, not a proposed production fix.

Signed-off-by: CJ Damsleth <kim@damsleth.no>
---
 drivers/nvme/host/apple.c | 5 +++--
 1 file changed, 3 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/nvme/host/apple.c b/drivers/nvme/host/apple.c
--- a/drivers/nvme/host/apple.c
+++ b/drivers/nvme/host/apple.c
@@ -1661,8 +1661,9 @@ static struct apple_nvme *apple_nvme_alloc(struct platform_device *pdev)
 	if (ret)
 		goto put_dev;
 
-	ret = devm_request_irq(anv->dev, anv->irq, apple_nvme_irq, 0,
-			       "nvme-apple", anv);
+	ret = devm_request_threaded_irq(anv->dev, anv->irq, NULL,
+					apple_nvme_irq, IRQF_ONESHOT,
+					"nvme-apple", anv);
 	if (ret) {
 		dev_err_probe(dev, ret, "Failed to request IRQ");
 		goto put_dev;
